  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nRachel's remnants brought widespread rainfall across the region. \nValleys and coastal areas west of the mountains saw near one half \ninch to over 2 inches of rainfall in total, with mountain areas \nreceiving 3-5\"+ of rainfall. Current radar mosaic depicts light \nshowers weakening over the area. Low level cumulus will continue to \nfunnel in off the ocean through afternoon, but most lower elevations \nshould see a brief lull in the rain. Coastal-facing slopes will see \nisolated light showers ongoing.\n\nA 550 mb low pressure over eastern Oregon will continue drop \nsouthward into the Great Basin on Monday and Tuesday. This will \nbring a continued cooler and wetter pattern to start the work week \nacross SoCal. As the low moves closer later tonight through Monday \nafternoon, we can expect an increase in coverage in light to locally \nmoderate showers. The best chance for showers will be along and west \nof the mountains. Accumulations will be light, with most valley and \ncoastal areas seeing less than one quarter of an inch of rainfall. \nThis system will also bring colder air with it, with 850mb \ntemperatures falling near 5-8C and 700mb temperatures falling near\n-3C. Snow levels will begin to drop further, most likely near 8000 \nft, giving some light snowfall to some of our highest peaks late \ntonight into Monday evening. Monday will be the coolest day overall \nas the weather system moves in. High temperatures will be in the 40s \nand 50s across the mountains, with 60s and 70s elsewhere. \nTemperatures by early Tuesday morning will be in the 40s and 50s for \nwestern valleys, closer to 60 degrees at the coast. Remnant clouds \nand light showers may linger into Tuesday morning west of the \nmountains, but mostly clear conditions should prevail by Tuesday \nafternoon.\n\nThe low pressure system will push eastward into the southern Rockies \nby Wednesday, as high pressure off the Pacific begins to expand over \nCalifornia. This will bring a drying and warming trend with flow \nswitching to weakly offshore. Temperatures will be closer to normal \nfor the coastal and western valley locations in the 70s to near 80 \ndegrees. By Thursday, models show a potential area of low pressure \nforming somewhere over the Desert Southwest as a ridge amplifies \nover the West Coast. The tighter gradient between these would create \na stronger offshore flow pattern, where increased Santa Ana winds \nwill become possible from the coastal slopes into the western \nvalleys. There is still low confidence on where the low tracks, \nwhich will help determine not only how strong winds will be, but \nalso how warm areas west of the mountains and how cool the mountains \nand deserts become.",
